  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing registration for climate migrants in Madagascar: a piloted approach</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate-induced migration is a major factor driving the rapid urbanization in Toliara, Madagascar, as individuals from the Great South region relocate due to prolonged droughts and food insecurity. This influx of &quot;climate migrants,&quot; which refers specifically to two key groups of migrants - internally displaced persons (IDPs) migrating in the context of climate related disasters and economic migrants migrating voluntarily from rural areas affected by climate change - coupled with the absence of an effective registration system, poses substantial challenges to local governments in planning and delivering essential public services such as housing, healthcare, and education, which impacts both climate migrants and host communities in destination areas. The aim of this pilot project is to contribute to strengthening the government&apos;s response to address climate related migration in the urban municipality of Toliara by enabling local authorities to pilot a new structured and inclusive approach to improve climate migrant registration.</narrative>
  </description>
